{"about":"9843f16c55a02ce422d04eb9057d52cb554dfe0052e19a3d19653ba96e18dc92","body":"The conjecture claims the preference is a 'rational decision' based solely on past performance, but this fails to explain why past survival is a rational basis for future action without implicitly assuming the uniformity of nature. If corroboration reports only history and guarantees nothing about the future, then preferring the better-corroborated theory over a worse one for practical application is functionally identical to acting as if the future will resemble the past, which is the very principle of induction Popper sought to reject. The 'methodological rule of thumb' described is indistinguishable in practice from inductive inference, rendering the semantic distinction empty.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The distinction between rational decision and inductive smuggle"}